This file lists the third-party components that are compiled into or shipped
with a MoLow build, the license each one carries, and what that license asks of
anyone redistributing a build. Version numbers match go.mod and
frontend/package.json at the time of writing; run `go list -m all` and read
frontend/bun.lock for the exact resolved set.

The MIT and BSD-3-Clause components only require that their copyright notice and
license text travel with the source, and that the license text travels with a
binary. Every one of them is vendored under the module cache and mirrored in the
Go module proxy, so the notice obligation is met by not stripping the licenses
from the source tree.

The three sections below list the copyleft/derived, Apache-2.0, and permissive
components included in MoLow.


Copyleft components
-------------------

### ytm-rs (GPL-3.0-only) and Kawarp (MIT)

The animated album-art background in MoLow is ported from ytm-rs
(https://github.com/grapedjuice/ytm-rs, licensed GPL-3.0-only):

- `frontend/src/lib/backdrop-gl.ts`: WebGL2 animated album-art shader ported
  from `ytm-rs` (`src/shader.rs`).
- `internal/artwork/backdrop.go`: 64x64 dark-tint and separable box-blur cover
  pipeline ported from `ytm-rs` (`src/art.rs`).
- `frontend/src/lib/components/PlayerBackdrop.svelte`: WebGL2 player backdrop
  host and texture crossfade loop used in `AlbumPlayer.svelte`.

ytm-rs's animated backdrop is based on Kawarp (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Better
Lyrics, https://github.com/better-lyrics). Keep this attribution and the MIT
copyright notice intact in any redistribution.

### github.com/tphakala/go-aac v0.7.1 (LGPL-2.1-or-later)

Reached through the engine's `decode` package, so it is linked into every build
that can play AAC, M4A or MP4.

go-aac is a derivative work of FFmpeg's LGPL-licensed AAC codec and cannot be
relicensed permissively. It is used unmodified, as a library, with no changes to
its source.

If you redistribute a MoLow binary, you must:

1. Ship the LGPL-2.1 text. It is at
   https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.txt and is also included in
   the go-aac distribution.
2. State that go-aac is used, under LGPL-2.1-or-later, and name the version.
3. Make the corresponding source available. Pointing at the upstream module at
   the pinned version satisfies this:
   https://github.com/tphakala/go-aac/tree/v0.7.1
4. Not impose terms that forbid reverse engineering for the purpose of debugging
   modifications to the LGPL component. GPL-3.0 places no such restriction, so a
   stock MoLow build already complies.

Go links statically, so there is no separate shared object to swap. If you need
to replace go-aac with your own build, rebuild MoLow with a `replace` directive
pointing at your copy; no source change is required.


Apache-2.0 component
--------------------

### github.com/ebitengine/oto/v3 v3.5.1 (Apache-2.0)

Reached through the engine's audio backend. Apache-2.0 is compatible with
GPL-3.0. Redistributing a binary requires shipping the Apache-2.0 license text
and preserving any NOTICE file oto ships.

oto is also what carries the platform audio bindings: WASAPI on Windows,
PulseAudio on Linux, CoreAudio on macOS.


Permissive components
---------------------

### github.com/ikawaha/kagome v2.11.1 and the IPADIC dictionary (MIT, with the mecab-ipadic notice)

The lyric romanizer (`internal/romanize`) uses kagome to segment Japanese lines
and the `ipa` dictionary package for the readings. Both are MIT.

The `ipa` package embeds mecab-ipadic-2.7.0-20070801, whose data is distributed
by NAIST and includes entries from ICOT Free Software. The upstream `NOTICE.txt`
in that module requires the ICOT NO WARRANTY section to travel with any
redistribution of the dictionary, so it must be reproduced in full wherever a
build is documented:

- https://github.com/ikawaha/kagome-dict/blob/master/ipa/NOTICE.txt

The dictionary is compiled into every binary, so a redistributor ships that
notice alongside the LGPL obligations above.

